call_details.response:
The board state admits only one empty cell that completes four-in-a-row for x: (2,4), lying on the diagonal already occupied by x at (5,1), (4,2) and (3,3). That cell is not yet legal; the unique move that makes it legal is column 4. Playing column 4, however, opens a forced win for o:

- x places at (3,4) (threatening (2,4));
- o must answer at (2,4) and thereby creates an immediate horizontal threat at the already-three-o row-1 window whose fourth cell is (1,4);
- x is forced to block at (1,4);
- o finishes at the newly-legal cell (0,4), completing the diagonal (0,4)-(1,3)-(2,2)-(3,1).

Consequently column 4 is losing.

All other columns except column 1 likewise lose, because they leave intact the open two-in-a-row of o on the diagonal (1,2)-(2,3). o can then occupy one end of that diagonal:

- o plays (0,1), creating an immediate threat at the opposite end (3,4);
- x must block at (3,4) (and thereby threatens (2,4));
- o blocks at (2,4) while simultaneously creating the horizontal threat at (1,4);
- x must block at (1,4);
- o wins at (0,4).

(The same line works against every first move other than column 1.)

Column 1 is the unique reply that occupies (0,1) and therefore destroys the open diagonal before o can use it. After that single defensive occupation:

- column 4 becomes poisoned for both players (the side that first drops into it immediately loses by the lines already shown);
- o possesses no other move that creates an immediate threat;
- the remaining legal columns (0,3,5,6) lead to a quiet mid-game in which x can still contest the residual winning windows.

Hence the only non-losing opening is column 1.

Final Answer: 1

